# ssd1306_dc_decode: works out the command/data tag an SSD1306 link's missing
# `dc` wire would have carried, by reading the commands themselves.
#
# A page command (0xB0..0xB7) says a draw is about to start, and how much: the
# rest of the page. A complete 0x21 and 0x22 window says the same. Everything
# else is a command with a known number of arguments, so an initialisation
# prefix may change from frame to frame and nothing here needs telling.
#
# What a command cannot say on this part is that a draw has been cut short, or
# that a window set once still applies, so a silence on the link returns the
# block to reading commands. After 0xAE a page command or window only sets the
# pointer, until the next silence or 0xAF. The rtl header has the limits. **No
# part has run this.** It is tested on a capture of the user's device booting,
# and the command table is quoted from the datasheet.
name ssd1306_dc_decode
version 1.1.0
license MIT
description "Recovers an SSD1306 link's command/data tag with no D/C wire, by decoding the command stream: page and window commands open a draw of known length, and a silence on the link recovers a lost place"

top ssd1306_dc_decode
target ice40
target ecp5
target xc7

source rtl/ssd1306_dc_decode.v

param COLUMNS int 128 1..512
param PAGES int 8 1..32
param IDLE_CLOCKS int 0 0..16777215
param PAGE_COLUMN_CMDS int 0 0..2
param COUNT_WIDTH int 16 1..32

port clk in
port rst_n in
port in_byte in 8
port in_valid in
port link_active in
port out_byte out 8
port out_is_data out
port out_valid out
port synced out
port drawing out
port page_draws out COUNT_WIDTH
port window_draws out COUNT_WIDTH
port short_draws out COUNT_WIDTH
port dropped_bytes out COUNT_WIDTH
